WEBVTT

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:16.213
I didn't build a global warehouse, nor did I hire an international team to sell globally.

00:00:16.213 --> 00:00:18.648
I used Amazon's Global Selling Program.

00:00:18.648 --> 00:00:23.112
When I expanded DTocs beyond the US, I kept it simple.

00:00:23.112 --> 00:00:27.577
I used my existing US FBA inventory to fulfill international orders.

00:00:27.577 --> 00:00:29.200
Now Amazon handled everything.

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:31.694
From shipping, duties, compliance, to delivery.

00:00:31.694 --> 00:00:37.512
Yes, the fees was a little higher, but I wasn't optimized for profits yet.

00:00:37.512 --> 00:00:39.590
I was optimized for learning.

00:00:39.590 --> 00:00:41.668
What I gained was huge.

00:00:41.668 --> 00:00:46.240
Brand awareness, real customer data, and early traction, especially in Canada.

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:51.752
Once I started seeing consistent orders from Canada, I knew the demand was real.

00:00:51.752 --> 00:00:54.115
That's when I went all in.

00:00:54.115 --> 00:01:01.595
I registered a Canadian entity, I got my tax ID and trademark in place, and went locally into FBA.

00:01:01.595 --> 00:01:06.320
Within the first year, we scaled to $100,000 in sales in Canada.

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:09.257
Now we're following the same playbook for Europe.

00:01:09.257 --> 00:01:14.029
Starting lean, validating demand, and registering the company's profiles and VAT registrations in

00:01:14.029 --> 00:01:16.598
France and Germany as our FBA hubs.

00:01:16.598 --> 00:01:19.535
Here is what I wish I knew earlier.

00:01:19.535 --> 00:01:21.738
Global selling is not a commitment.

00:01:21.738 --> 00:01:23.573
It is a testing tool.

00:01:23.573 --> 00:01:25.409
Use it like market research.

00:01:25.409 --> 00:01:28.712
Let the data guide to where to invest next.

00:01:28.712 --> 00:01:32.016
And don't go all in until demand proves itself.

00:01:32.016 --> 00:01:35.320
This is how you expand globally without the headache.
